.TH MAN 1 "2026" "ntlibc" "ntlibc User Commands"
.SH NAME
man \- find and display a manual page
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B man
.RB [ \-k ]
.I name
.RI [ name ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B man
.RI [ section ]
.I name
.RI [ name ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B man
writes information about each
.I name
operand: for a standard utility, its synopsis, options, and operands
at minimum.
.PP
This is a real parser and formatter for the
.B man
troff macro package subset (not full troff/groff) \(em see this
project's own
.B src/util/man.c
for the exact, cited boundary of what is and is not implemented.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-k
Interpret
.I name
operands as keywords: search every page's NAME line under
.B $MANPATH
and print each match as
.IR name (section)
\- description.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I section
A manual section, restricting the search (e.g.
.B man 3 printf
looks only in section 3). A common, near-universal extension; not
part of the base POSIX synopsis, which is only
.BR "man [\-k] name..." .
.TP
.I name
A keyword (with
.BR \-k )
or standard utility name.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MANPATH
Colon-separated list of directories to search, each containing
.BR man1 / ,
.BR man2 / ,
and so on. Two special cases are worth calling out:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
If unset or empty, the fallback is
.BR /usr/share/man:/usr/local/share/man .
.IP \(bu 2
Harmless on a platform with no such convention (this project's own
NT target): the search simply finds nothing there, the same clean
"no manual entry" a fresh, unconfigured Linux machine would also
give.
.RE
.TP
.B MANSECT
Colon-separated default section search order, used when no
.I section
operand is given. Default:
.BR 1:2:3:4:5:6:7:8:9 .
.TP
.B PAGER
The pager program to page output through, when standard output is a
terminal. If unset, a minimal built-in
.B \-\-More\-\-
pager is used instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred (no such page, or \-k found nothing).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR true (1),
.BR sh (1)
